      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a pure lottery offers statistical fairness, it is not the only consensus definition of equity in housing policy, as most experts distinguish \"equality\" from \"equity.\" The prevailing professional view is that true equity requires prioritizing those with the most severe needs to correct historical and systemic disparities, rather than treating everyone as if they stand at the same starting line. A consensus exists that \"fairness\" often depends on outcomes, not just equal chances; allowing the most solvent to \"win\" a lottery while the most in need cannot afford the rent perpetuates inequality. Therefore, most policy advocates argue that a need-based or hybrid model is ethically superior and more just than a neutral lottery that ignores the severity of housing deprivation.",
